   },
   "Italian Fascism": {
-    "d": "Holds that the state embodies the nation: totalism and ethical statism, corporatism joining workers and employers under a single party and an autocratic leader, centralism, romantic nationalism and imperialism. It rejects representative democracy, referendum democracy, civil libertarianism, permissivism, laissez-faire, communization, state socialism, cosmopolitanism, internationalism and non-interventionism.",
-    "s": "totalism:0.98|ethical_statism:1|organic_statism:1|corporatism:0.84|state_corporatism:0.9|syndicracy:0.6|romantic_nationalism:0.8|cultural_nationalism:0.83|Imperialism:0.9|centralism:0.8|partocracy:0.34|autocracy:0.73|regimentalism:0.7|industrialism:0.5|futurism:0.67|representative_democracy:-1|referendum_democracy:-0.7|civil_liberalism:-1|civil_libertarianism:-1|permissivism:-0.8|laissez_faire:-1|state_socialism:-0.8|communization:-1|Cosmopolitanism:-1|Internationalism:-1|Non_Interventionism:-0.8|pacifism:-1|reputationalism:-0.6|patchwork:-0.7|anti_organizationalism:-0.7|panarchy:-0.6|syndicalism:0.62|ethnic_nationalism:-0.68|racial_nationalism:-0.87|alternative_modernism:0.78|reactionary_modernism:0.6|hierarchical_communism:0.76|religious_nationalism:-0.45|pan_nationalism:-0.23|protectionism:0.8|absolute_idealism:0.65|actual_idealism:0.95|individualism:-0.9|pluralism:-0.8|deontology:-0.7|naturalism:-0.61|existentialism:-0.6|monism:0.6|idealism:0.5"
+    "d": "The state absorbs the individual, because no person, party, union or class may stand outside it, so that everything lies within the state, nothing outside it and nothing against it (Totalism). The state is a spiritual and ethical reality, a synthesis of all values, which gives the individual freedom and a higher purpose by raising him into the life of the nation (Ethical Statism). The state is a living organism of the whole society, in which classes, regions and institutions are organs with duties under one authority, because the individual finds his purpose in serving the unity of the nation (Organic Statism). Workers and employers are grouped by trade in syndicates and corporations recognized by the state, so that the struggle between classes gives way to the coordination of interests in the unity of the state (Corporatism). The syndicates and corporations are created and controlled by the state, which licenses one body per trade and holds its labour and its capital to national aims, because the state alone can unite their interests (State Corporatism). Delegates are drawn from the syndicates and corporations in place of party representatives, since the producers organized in their trades should form the national chamber (Syndicracy). The nation is a moral and historical community with a will and a mission of its own, whose destiny asks sacrifice of its people (Romantic Nationalism). The nation is carried by tradition, language and customs that link the generations, because outside history man is nothing (Cultural Nationalism). A strong nation extends its power over other territories, since the will to empire is a sign of vitality and a people that does not expand declines (Imperialism). Authority is concentrated in the national government, because a unitary state enforces one will uniformly over all provinces and sub-national divisions (Centralism). One party dominates the political process and the state apparatus, so that it directs the whole nation rather than competing representatives (Partocracy). One leader holds absolute power without external legal restraints, since decisive personal command is necessary to carry out the national will (Autocracy). Civil life is organized and disciplined like a militia, because the citizen is a soldier of the nation and life is a duty and a struggle (Regimentalism). Modern industry and machines are embraced as the means of national power and renewal, since a strong state needs productive might (Industrialism). The restless energy of speed, machines and youth is praised, because a vigorous nation renews itself without being bound by the habits of a tired order (Futurism). Voting for elected representatives who govern on behalf of citizens is rejected, because it equates the nation with the majority and splits it into quarrelling parties (against Representative Democracy). Direct votes on laws through referendums are rejected, since the crowd is not fit to decide the state's ends (against Referendum Democracy). The protection of individual rights against the state is rejected, because liberty exists only in the state and for the individual within it (against Civil Liberalism). Safeguarding personal privacy and freedom of speech against the state is rejected, because individual rights cannot stand above the nation (against Civil Libertarianism). Consensual adult conduct and private vices are not exempt from the state, which rejects moral neutrality because lifestyle choices shape the national character (against Permissivism). Free private transactions without government intervention are rejected since the economy must serve national power (against Laissez-Faire). State ownership run by central planning is rejected, because it ends private profit through class doctrine instead of through national unity (against State Socialism). An immediate abolition of the state and classes by revolution is rejected, since the nation must stay above class (against Communization). Belonging to a single community of all human beings is rejected, because loyalty belongs to the nation and its morality does not transcend borders (against Cosmopolitanism). Cooperation among nations through treaties is rejected since the national interest cannot be bound by joint commitments (against Internationalism). Avoiding alliances and wars is rejected because a vigorous state should take part in wars of expansion (against Non-Interventionism). Disarmament and nonviolent resistance are rejected, since perpetual peace is neither possible nor useful and war alone raises all human energy to its highest tension (against Pacifism). Order kept by peer accountability and ostracism through community trust networks is rejected, because only the state's police and law enforce discipline (against Reputationalism). Splitting territory into many seceding city-states is rejected, since the nation must remain one sovereign whole (against Patchwork). Fluid, spontaneous association without permanent institutions is rejected because fixed structures and formal organization give the nation its form (against Anti-Organizationalism). Several competing governments sharing one territory by free choice are rejected, because one sovereign state must hold the whole nation (against Panarchy).",
+    "s": "totalism:0.98|ethical_statism:1|organic_statism:1|corporatism:0.84|state_corporatism:0.9|syndicracy:0.6|romantic_nationalism:0.8|cultural_nationalism:0.83|Imperialism:0.9|centralism:0.8|partocracy:0.34|autocracy:0.73|regimentalism:0.7|industrialism:0.5|futurism:0.67|representative_democracy:-1|referendum_democracy:-0.7|civil_liberalism:-1|civil_libertarianism:-1|permissivism:-0.8|laissez_faire:-1|state_socialism:-0.8|communization:-1|Cosmopolitanism:-1|Internationalism:-1|Non_Interventionism:-0.8|pacifism:-1|reputationalism:-0.6|patchwork:-0.7|anti_organizationalism:-0.7|panarchy:-0.6|syndicalism:0.62|ethnic_nationalism:-0.68|racial_nationalism:-0.87|alternative_modernism:0.78|reactionary_modernism:0.6|hierarchical_communism:0.76|religious_nationalism:-0.45|pan_nationalism:-0.23|protectionism:0.8|absolute_idealism:0.65|actual_idealism:0.95|individualism:-0.9|pluralism:-0.8|deontology:-0.7|naturalism:-0.61|existentialism:-0.6|monism:0.6|idealism:0.5|reformism:-0.6"
   },
   "Evolianism": {
-    "d": "A Traditionalist outlook that sees modern equality, democracy and materialism as decline, and seeks an order led by spiritual authority and a warrior aristocracy.",
-    "s": "perennial_traditionalism:1|aristocracy:1|feudalism:0.5|reactionaryism:0.7|representative_democracy:-1|progressivism:-1|social_progressivism:-1|civil_liberalism:-1|laissez_faire:-1|state_socialism:-1|communization:-1|amoralism:-0.7|ochlocracy:-0.7|organic_statism:0.6|positivism:-0.8|individualism:-0.58|idealism:0.9|materialism:-0.9|stoicism:0.9|dualism:0.8|naturalism:-0.8|utilitarianism:-0.6"
+    "d": "The highest authority is a single primordial tradition and its sacred metaphysical hierarchy, which affirms an ancient wisdom shared by the civilizations of the past, since spirit stands above matter (Perennial Traditionalism). Power belongs to a distinguished ruling class of inherited status and education, whose warrior honour and long-term stake in the land qualify it to rule (Aristocracy). The medieval order of lords, vassals and personal loyalty bound by land is admired because it joined the sacred to military service (Feudalism). A return to a previous political state of society is sought, because modern developments are a decline that must be undone (Reactionaryism). Rule by the elected representatives of equal voters is rejected, since the numbers of the crowd cannot judge what is highest (against Representative Democracy). The belief in advance through science and reform is rejected, because it mistakes the loss of tradition for improvement of the human condition (against Progressivism). Using government and social action to remedy inequality and discrimination is rejected, since equality denies natural rank (against Social-Progressivism). The protection of individual rights and expression against any authority is rejected because it frees persons from the duty and order that give them a place (against Civil Liberalism). Free markets where profit and trade govern are rejected since commerce stands below honour and service (against Laissez-Faire). State ownership managed centrally for material production is rejected, because it builds society on matter instead of spirit (against State Socialism). A revolution abolishing classes and money is rejected since it destroys every hierarchy and sacred rank (against Communization). An ethic without fixed commandments, resting only on desire, is rejected because a person without a rule has no form (against Amoralism). Unrestrained majority rule driven by mass passions is rejected since it hands power to the lowest elements of society (against Ochlocracy).",
+    "s": "perennial_traditionalism:1|aristocracy:1|feudalism:0.5|reactionaryism:0.7|representative_democracy:-1|progressivism:-1|social_progressivism:-1|civil_liberalism:-1|laissez_faire:-1|state_socialism:-1|communization:-1|amoralism:-0.7|ochlocracy:-0.7|organic_statism:0.6|positivism:-0.8|individualism:-0.58|idealism:0.9|materialism:-0.9|stoicism:0.9|dualism:0.8|naturalism:-0.8|utilitarianism:-0.6|reformism:-0.7"
   },
   "Futurism": {
-    "d": "Celebrates speed, machines, industry and constant change. Industrialism and Promethean mastery over nature drive an alternative modernism and energetic national expansion (imperialism), while conservatism, traditionalist conservatism, reactionaryism, primitivism and neo-Luddism are rejected as reverence for the past.",
-    "s": "industrialism:1|prometheanism:0.6|alternative_modernism:0.7|Imperialism:0.6|conservatism:-1|traditionalist_conservatism:-1|reactionaryism:-1|primitivism:-1|neoluddism:-1|convivialism:-0.6|futurism:0.9|actual_idealism:0.4|thomism:-0.51|dualism:-0.51|materialism:0.5|pragmatism:0.5|stoicism:-0.5|naturalism:0.48|deontology:-0.4"
+    "d": "Speed, large-scale manufacturing and machines are the beauty of the age, because industry maximizes energy, infrastructure and material abundance for a people (Industrialism). Humanity should achieve total mastery over nature through technology, so that scarcity and old limits are overcome by human will (Prometheanism). A path to modernity of its own, outside the status quo and distinct from the dominant model, is needed because the new age must break with museums and the past (Alternative Modernism). A vigorous nation extends its power and dominance over other territories through force, since daring shows itself by expansion (Imperialism). Practices, institutions and accumulated wisdom that resist rapid change are rejected because they hold back the restless advance of the new (against Conservatism). Inherited customs and local piety are rejected since reverence for the past blocks the energy of the present (against Traditionalist Conservatism). A return to a previous political order is rejected because the future cannot be reached by looking backward (against Reactionaryism). Dismantling technological civilization to return to wild hunter-gatherer life is rejected, since it would give up the machine that makes the age great (against Primitivism). Opposing automated, hyper-industrial expansion in favour of local craft is rejected, because slow human-scale labour would hold back production and speed (against Neo-Luddism). Repairable, small-scale tools kept to personal creativity and local autonomy are rejected since a gentle, decentralized technology cannot match the power of large machines (against Convivialism).",
+    "s": "industrialism:1|prometheanism:0.6|alternative_modernism:0.7|Imperialism:0.6|conservatism:-1|traditionalist_conservatism:-1|reactionaryism:-1|primitivism:-1|neoluddism:-1|convivialism:-0.6|futurism:0.9|actual_idealism:0.4|thomism:-0.51|dualism:-0.51|materialism:0.5|pragmatism:0.5|stoicism:-0.5|naturalism:0.48|deontology:-0.4|reformism:-0.6|revolutionism:0.6"
   },
   "National-Socialism": {
     "d": "A racial-nationalist doctrine holding that the nation is a community of blood, that races are unequal, and that the state exists to preserve and expand the German or Aryan people under a single leader and party. Society is to be organized as a unified people's community in which all institutions are brought into line with the party under the leader principle, and individual rights and class interests are subordinate to the race and the nation. Private enterprise is permitted only so far as it serves state goals, and the economy is directed toward rearmament, self-sufficiency and expansion; territory (living space) is to be won for the race through war. Supporters oppose liberal democracy, Marxism, internationalism and Jews, whom they regard as the race's enemy.",
