   "Agrarian Fascism": {
-    "d": "Joins fascist state authority to a rural, peasant-based vision of society: holds that the strength of the nation lies in farmland, village life and tradition, directed by a strong leader and a disciplined state. Supporters oppose urban industrial liberalism and communism.",
+    "d": "Human life is centred on farming, land stewardship and rural communities, because decentralized cultivation of the soil rears a people with roots (Agrarianism). Land is redistributed to the peasantry and farmed collectively, since the rural worker, not the city, is the foundation of the nation (Agrarian Socialism). A single strong leader holds absolute power over the countryside, because village life needs decisive personal command (Autocracy). The state is a moral organism that unites individuals and gives the nation its spiritual purpose, so that the peasantry serves it as its highest end (Ethical Statism). The nation is an organic spirit born in folklore and ancestral land, since the soil carries its heroic soul (Romantic Nationalism). Inherited customs, natural obligations and local piety are preserved against rapid modernization, because rural tradition holds communal bonds together (Traditionalist Conservatism). Public etiquette, order and behavioural standards are enforced with discipline, since strength and decorum mark a rural, orderly people (Disciplinarianism). Large-scale manufacturing and urban extraction are rejected because they uproot the peasant and drain the countryside (against Industrialism). Individual rights set against the community are rejected, since urban liberal freedom loosens village bonds (against Civil Liberalism). Elected representatives who legislate on behalf of voters are rejected because city parties do not speak for the farming people (against Representative Democracy). Central ownership of production by planners is rejected, since it would take the land from the peasant family (against State Socialism).",
     "s": "agrarianism:1|agrarian_socialism:0.6|autocracy:0.8|ethical_statism:0.6|romantic_nationalism:0.8|traditionalist_conservatism:0.7|disciplinarianism:0.6|industrialism:-0.6|civil_liberalism:-1|representative_democracy:-1|state_socialism:-0.8|individualism:-0.68|naturalism:-0.68|idealism:0.61|existentialism:-0.56|pluralism:-0.55|rationalism:-0.54|stoicism:0.53|utilitarianism:-0.53|dualism:0.49"
   },
   "Strasserism": {
-    "d": "The socialist wing of National Socialism: guild-socialist and social-corporatist organization of worker guilds and nationalized key industries, ethical statism and racial nationalism. It rejects laissez-faire, communization, civil liberalism, representative democracy, cosmopolitanism and internationalism, and opposes big capital.",
-    "s": "guild_socialism:0.8|social_corporatism:0.6|state_socialism:0.4|racial_nationalism:0.9|ethical_statism:0.6|laissez_faire:-1|communization:-1|civil_liberalism:-1|representative_democracy:-1|Cosmopolitanism:-1|Internationalism:-1|hierarchical_communism:0.5|multiculturalism:-0.8|organic_statism:0.5|protectionism:0.8|historical_materialism:-0.3|thomism:0.36|individualism:-0.82|deontology:-0.65|pluralism:-0.64|existentialism:-0.54|monism:0.53|utilitarianism:-0.51|rationalism:-0.47"
+    "d": "Each trade should be governed by its own national guild of craftsmen, because the nationalized key industries need producers who answer to the nation (Guild Socialism). The state arranges industrial sectors so that capital works with labour, because workers' syndicates gain a share in every decision of their sector (Social-Corporatism). Firms pass from private owners to their workers, with skilled managers chosen by merit, so that the national economy is run by those who know it (Hierarchical Communism). The nation is defined by race and descent, because the working people form a community of blood (Racial Nationalism). The state is the nation's conscience, which lifts every citizen above private interest because only in it do people reach their highest duties (Ethical Statism). A market left free of regulation, subsidies and tariffs is rejected, since private transactions without government let profit exploit the worker (against Laissez-Faire). The immediate abolition of the state and classes by revolution is rejected, since it ignores the nation as the frame of social justice (against Communization). The protection of individual rights and expression against the community is rejected because liberal freedom serves property (against Civil Liberalism). Elected representatives who govern on behalf of voters are rejected, since parliamentary parties serve capital and not the workers (against Representative Democracy). A single human community with shared morality that transcends borders is rejected, because the worker's loyalty is to the nation (against Cosmopolitanism). Cooperation among nations through treaties is rejected since the nation's socialism must be built within its own borders (against Internationalism). Coexisting communities with separate customs inside one state are rejected because they break the unity of the people (against Multiculturalism).",
+    "s": "guild_socialism:0.8|social_corporatism:0.6|state_socialism:0.4|racial_nationalism:0.9|ethical_statism:0.6|laissez_faire:-1|communization:-1|civil_liberalism:-1|representative_democracy:-1|Cosmopolitanism:-1|Internationalism:-1|hierarchical_communism:0.5|multiculturalism:-0.8|organic_statism:0.5|protectionism:0.8|historical_materialism:-0.3|thomism:0.36|individualism:-0.82|deontology:-0.65|pluralism:-0.64|existentialism:-0.54|monism:0.53|utilitarianism:-0.51|rationalism:-0.47|revolutionism:0.7"
