   "Falangism": {
-    "d": "Spanish national-syndicalist doctrine of a single national state: corporatism in vertical syndicates joining workers and employers, ethical statism in which the state embodies the nation's moral unity, romantic nationalism of the fatherland's destiny, centralism and regimentalism, traditionalist conservatism of Catholic tradition, and an imperial Hispanic mission. It rejects civil liberalism, representative democracy, class struggle (communization and state socialism), laissez-faire, regionalism and confederalism.",
-    "s": "corporatism:0.9|traditionalist_conservatism:0.6|ethical_statism:0.7|romantic_nationalism:0.8|Imperialism:0.5|centralism:0.8|regimentalism:0.6|regionalism:-0.8|confederalism:-0.8|laissez_faire:-0.8|civil_liberalism:-1|representative_democracy:-1|state_socialism:-0.8|communization:-1|patchwork:-0.7|organic_statism:0.7|panarchy:-0.6|state_corporatism:0.8|protectionism:0.8|absolute_idealism:0.59|individualism:-0.84|idealism:0.8|materialism:-0.8|nihilism:-0.8|naturalism:-0.74|pluralism:-0.64|monism:0.61|stoicism:0.6"
+    "d": "Society is organized into vertical syndicates that join workers and employers, because functional bodies, not classes, express the common interests of producers (Corporatism). Inherited customs, natural obligations and the Catholic piety of the people are preserved, since the nation's historic institutions should resist rapid modernization (Traditionalist Conservatism). The state is the moral unity of the nation, a spiritual entity that unites individuals and realizes a higher common destiny (Ethical Statism). The fatherland is a destiny in history, animated by an ancestral soul and heroic mythology, so that Spain has a universal mission (Romantic Nationalism). The nation extends its influence over other territories through the force of empire, because it has a mission among the peoples of its language and culture (Imperialism). Authority is concentrated in the national government with provinces as administrative divisions, since a unitary state enforces one national order (Centralism). Civilian life is mobilized through mandatory service and youth programs, because discipline directs everyone towards national goals (Regimentalism). Autonomous historical regions with their own parliaments are rejected since separatism fractures the unity of the fatherland (against Regionalism). A union of sovereign regions formed by treaty is rejected, because the nation cannot be a compact of parts that may veto the whole (against Confederalism). Free private transactions without state intervention are rejected since the economy must serve national unity (against Laissez-Faire). The protection of individual rights and freedom of expression is rejected because liberalism isolates the person from the community (against Civil Liberalism). Elected representatives who govern for rival parties are rejected, since parties divide the fatherland (against Representative Democracy). State ownership managed centrally for class interests is rejected because it turns the nation into a class dictatorship (against State Socialism). A revolution to abolish classes and money is rejected, since it breaks the national community (against Communization). Splitting territory into seceding city-states is rejected because the homeland is one and indivisible (against Patchwork).",
