+    "d": "The Arab nation is one people split by borders, so that its scattered groups of one language and culture must be unified into a single state (Pan-Nationalism). The state owns the means of production and directs the economy centrally, because socialism ends exploitation and develops the nation's resources (State Socialism). A single party leads the state apparatus and the political process, since the vanguard of the nation should govern in place of rival representatives (Partocracy). Profits and resource rents of state-owned enterprises supply the main revenue for public spending, so that oil and industry serve the people (State Enterprise Revenue). Private markets free of government direction are rejected because they leave the nation's wealth to foreign and domestic exploiters (against Laissez-Faire). Rule by religious law and a deity's clergy is rejected, since the nation's identity is secular and Arab and not defined by one sect (against Theocracy).",
+    "s": "pan_nationalism:1|state_socialism:0.6|partocracy:0.8|laissez_faire:-0.8|theocracy:-0.7|progressivism:0.4|Imperialism:-0.4|state_enterprise_revenue:0.7|individualism:-0.37|naturalism:0.41|idealism:0.4|pragmatism:0.4|deontology:-0.3|monism:0.25|dualism:-0.22|stoicism:0.2|revolutionism:0.6|coupism:0.9"
